            stem: "A citizen of State A sued a citizen of State A in a federal district court on a state-law contract claim for $200,000, alleging, mistakenly, that the defendant is a citizen of State B. The defendant answered, admitted the allegation, litigated for two years and lost at trial. On appeal, the defendant argued for the first time that both parties are citizens of State A and that no basis for federal jurisdiction exists.",
            call: "How should the court of appeals rule?",
            options: [
              { text: "Vacate the judgment and direct dismissal, because subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be conferred by consent and may be raised at any time.", why: "Rule 12(h)(3): the court must dismiss whenever it determines that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction; it is never waived or forfeited." },
              { text: "Affirm the judgment, because the defendant waived any objection to jurisdiction by not raising it in its first Rule 12 response.", why: "Only the defenses in Rule 12(b)(2)-(5) are lost by omission; lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is not.", trap: 5 },
              { text: "Affirm the judgment, because by litigating through trial in the federal court the defendant consented to the court's jurisdiction.", why: "Consent can supply personal jurisdiction, but not subject-matter jurisdiction.", trap: 4 },
              { text: "Affirm the judgment, because the defendant admitted the citizenship allegation, and an admission in the answer binds it on appeal.", why: "Parties cannot create jurisdiction by admission; the court must look at the real citizenship of the parties.", trap: 5 },
            ],
            answer: 0,
            facts: ["mistakenly, that the defendant is a citizen of State B", "for the first time"],
            say: "Subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be waived, consented to or forfeited, and it can be raised for the first time on appeal.",
